findings.body:
The HPEXPIREAT propagation builds its argument vector in `robj *argv[5]` (src/t_hash.c:2306) but writes six elements: indices 0 through 5, with `argv[5] = c->argv[2]` at line 2312. It then passes `6` as the count at line 2313. The `argv[5]` store is one slot past the end of the array, which is a stack buffer overflow on every HINCRBYFLOAT of a field that has a TTL. The intended six-element command (HPEXPIREAT key ms FIELDS 1 field) is correct, only the declared size is wrong.

This is undefined behavior. It can corrupt adjacent locals, such as `expireat`, `buf`, or saved state, and it will trip AddressSanitizer or stack-protector builds. The replication test may still pass on a normal build by luck, so it does not prove the code is safe.

::diff{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2298-2314"}

::callout{type="security"}
An out-of-bounds stack write on a client-triggerable command path is a memory-safety bug, not only a logic error. It must be fixed before merge.

Fix by sizing the array to match the number of arguments:

::suggestion{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2306-2313"}
robj *argv[6];
        argv[0] = shared.hpexpireat;
        argv[1] = c->argv[1];
        argv[2] = expireObj;
        argv[3] = shared.fields;
        argv[4] = shared.integers[1];
        argv[5] = c->argv[2];
        alsoPropagate(c->db->id, argv, 6, PROPAGATE_AOF|PROPAGATE_REPL);
